Eitri @VERSION@ -- Your Neovim, with Claude Code beside it.
Built from commit @COMMIT@ in the Ubuntu 24.04 release container. It runs on glibc 2.39, GTK 4.14
and WebKitGTK 2.40 (the webkitgtk-6.0 API) or newer.

0.2.2 is a small release: bypass tabs now approve every question Claude Code asks, the trust question says what
a project's allow rules still need, two hardening fixes, and a user guide. Much of the work since 0.2.1 is
groundwork for a macOS app and is not meant to change anything on Linux.

## What's new in 0.2.2

**The agent**

- **Bypass approves every question Claude Code asks itself.** In 0.2.1 a question that your own
  `permissions.ask` rule forced, or one that gave no reason, stayed a card even in a bypass tab. Now a bypass tab
  answers all of them with no card, and the tool's row says `allowed in bypass`; entering bypass approves such
  waiting questions with the other cards. So in bypass an ask rule stops nothing (known issues). Auto tabs are
  unchanged: there those questions are still cards of their own.
- **The trust question says what a project's allow rules still need.** When it lists `permissions.allow`
  entries, it now adds that Claude Code applies them only if the folder is also trusted in terminal `claude`
  (Eitri never sets that). Hooks, MCP servers and deny or ask rules load with `y` either way.

**Hardening**

- `VERDANDI_CLAUDE_EXTRA_MCP_SERVERS`, a variable that newer agent backends read to add MCP servers to every
  session, is removed from the backend's environment, so a value in your shell cannot reach an Eitri session.
  Eitri adds no MCP servers of its own; those your settings and a trusted project's configuration name still load.
- The agent panel loads its page only when it is sure where the page's script policy goes; otherwise it shows a
  short page saying the panel could not be built. A release build always passes this check.
- The panel's text fields no longer auto-capitalise, autocorrect or spell-check what you type.

**Documentation**

- A user guide in the repository, `docs/guide/` (in English and Chinese): getting started, keys,
  configuration, permissions, turn review and companion mode.
- INSTALL is shorter, and the AUR package comes right after the quick install.

**Under the hood**

- The agent panel's logic moved into a library of its own with no GUI toolkit, and the Neovide fork gained hooks
  for an application that hosts Neovide's own window. Both are groundwork for a macOS app, which is not usable
  yet. On Linux nothing is meant to change.

## Upgrading from 0.2.1

- **Nothing to rebuild.** The agent backend is pinned to the same revision as in 0.2.1, so `install.sh` keeps
  the one you have, and after a `.deb`/`.rpm` upgrade `eitri setup` has nothing to do.
- **If you relied on an ask rule inside a bypass tab,** it no longer stops anything there; use an Auto tab.

**`v0.2.0` will stop building from git.** The public history of the Neovide fork was rewritten after 0.2.0
(only commit metadata changed; the files are the same), so the `v0.2.0` tag and every earlier public commit
point at fork commits that no branch holds any more. Build 0.2.1 or later from git. For 0.2.0 itself, its
own `install.sh` (the 0.2.0 release asset) with `--from-source` works only until GitHub stops serving those
old objects, and its `eitri-0.2.0-source.tar.gz` release asset, which carries the fork's files, rebuilds it
for good; later releases' `install.sh` cannot build 0.2.0 from source. The 0.2.0 packages, tarball and
`install.sh` are not affected.

## How much of this has been tried

Everything new has automated tests, and the bypass change was also run against the real `claude` (2.1.288).
The panel after its move was seen working in an isolated, headless test session (scripted keys, not a person
at a real desktop): the page, streaming, reload, the trust question, turn review, the keys, links, the
companion window and recovery from a crashed web process. The bypass change and the trust question's new note
have not been seen on a screen yet.

What the 0.2.1 notes said about its own features still holds: companion mode, `eitri split`, the GNOME Shell
extension, turn review, the trust question and Auto tabs on Claude Code's own auto mode have been seen working
only in that kind of isolated session, and Hyprland, niri, KDE and a real GNOME login with the extension have
not been tried. None of it has been through real hardware and a person's hands yet; 0.3.0 waits for that and
for a week of daily use. What you may run into is listed in the known issues (`docs/known-issues.md` in the
repository).

## Performance, against native Neovide

Typing is within 1 ms of native Neovide at the median and at the 99th percentile on the two machines
measured (an Intel laptop and an AMD desktop, GTK 4.22.5, 60 and 165 Hz; measured just before 0.2.0). Below
GTK 4.16, and while a reply is streaming, see the known issues. **One known gap:** at 165 Hz on the Intel
laptop, with other work keeping the machine 17-28 % busy, the editor drew 137-149 frames a second where
native Neovide drew 160 (86-93 %), and 111 (69 %) at 34 % busy, with one 83.6 ms frame in four runs. That was
measured before 0.2.0's typing-latency fix and has not been measured again since.

## Install

    curl --proto '=https' --proto-redir '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSfL https://github.com/HunterGrey-cyber/eitri/releases/download/v@VERSION@/install.sh | sh -s -- --version @VERSION@

or install `eitri_@VERSION@_amd64.deb` / `eitri-@VERSION@-1.x86_64.rpm` and run `eitri setup`
once, or install from `eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz` with
`sh install.sh --tarball eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz --sums SHA256SUMS --sig SHA256SUMS.sig`
(or unpack it and run its own `bin/eitri setup` from inside the extracted directory).

Eitri needs `nvim` >= 0.10 somewhere it can find one -- either on `PATH`, or its own private copy
under `XDG_DATA_HOME/eitri/nvim` (never placed on `PATH`; none of the packages depend on a system
nvim). Ubuntu 24.04's own package is 0.9.5, below that floor. `install.sh` (the curl command above)
offers to fetch the official nvim release for you at install time if none new enough is found; run
`eitri setup --nvim-only` on its own at any later time -- after a `.deb`/`.rpm`/tarball install,
or again -- to get the same offer.

## The agent backend is built on your machine

No asset here contains Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK. `eitri setup` downloads it from npm and
builds the agent backend (the sidecar) on your own machine, from `@VERDANDI_ASSET@` (the public
Verdandi at @VERDANDI_REV@) and the official Node release pinned in `RELEASE`. The SDK is not open
source; its own licence applies to your copy of it, and `eitri setup` shows where to read it.

## Licences and source

This program statically links nvim-rs @NVIM_RS_VERSION@, which is licensed under the GNU LGPL-3.0;
its use is covered by that licence. The Corresponding Source for this release is
`@SOURCE_ASSET@` on this page -- **not** GitHub's automatic "Source code" archives, which lack the
Neovide fork submodule, the vendored Rust dependencies and the Skia archive the build linked. It
rebuilds offline, and `SOURCE` (installed at `share/licenses/eitri/SOURCE`) gives the exact recipe
for relinking against a modified nvim-rs. `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES`, beside it, carries every other
notice.

